    "index": 12,
    "markdown": "6 Which of the two weighs more, Cyril or Maisie?\nProbably . . .\n7 Who'll come with me to the Zoo? (We all . . .)\n8 Who used to live in this old house?\n9 Who dares to jump over this stream? Nobody . . .\n10 What's the capital of France?\n11 Who saw Maisie in the town yesterday?\n12 Which of you knows the shortest way to the station?\n13 How many of you ought to be in bed by now?\n14 How many of you have breakfast before seven in the morning?\n15 Who likes chocolate?\n16 Who came late today?\n17 Who taught you to swim?\n18 How many of you ought to do more homework?\nWe all . . .\n19 Who can answer my questions? (Most of us . . .)\n20 Which takes longer to say: Ightham or Llanfair-pwllgwyngyllgogerwchwyndrobbwllllandyrilio-gogogoch?\n\n19.5\n\nElementary\nAGREEMENT\n\nSee also Exercise 19.20.\n\nThe auxiliaries are commonly used when we agree with someone:\n\nusing yes for simple agreement.\n\nIt's very hot today. - Yes, it is.\n\nusing so for surprise.\n\nYour glass is empty. - So it is.\n\nusing of course, etc., for something obvious.\n\nThey say we shall win. - Of course we shall.\n\nThe teacher reads the statement and the student responds.\n\n* * *\n\n● Agree with the following remarks:\n1 The door is shut. So . . .\n2 Mr Smith is sitting down. Yes . . .\n3 That music sounds pleasant. Yes . . .\n4 You've got some ink on your sleeve. So . . .\n5 You are all learning English very quickly. Of course . . .",
